WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.687
A multiclass model produces one score per class. Softmax exponentiates these scores and divides by their

00:00:07.687 --> 00:00:15.174
sum, making a positive distribution across mutually exclusive classes. Increasing one score therefore changes more than

00:00:15.174 --> 00:00:16.110
one probability.

00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:22.233
These scores can be any real values. This softmax model assumes exactly one of the classes

00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:23.283
is the target.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:31.980
Subtracting the maximum score before exponentiating gives the same probabilities and avoids unnecessarily large exponentials.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.920
If the cat score rises while the other scores stay fixed, what happens to their probabilities?

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.630
Pause here and make a prediction before continuing.

00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:52.447
Cross entropy with a one-hot target is the negative logarithm of the chosen true-class probability. For

00:00:52.447 --> 00:00:58.473
our starting inputs, Cat: 0.42232. Dog: 0.42232. Background: 0.15536.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.798
Increasing the cat score lowers this loss. The coupled probabilities sum to one, so increasing one

00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:06.610
class changes the others too.

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:15.310
Use stable log-softmax calculations for extreme scores. Independent multilabel outcomes need a different model than this

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:16.843
mutually exclusive softmax.

